{
  "id": "code:md-render",
  "tier": 6,
  "domain": "text",
  "title": "Give mdlite nested lists, reference links, and a code fence that escapes",
  "prompt": "`mdlite` is the repository in this workspace: `README.md` describes what it already does, `src/` holds it, and `test/mdlite.test.js` is its own suite, which passes. Close the three gaps its README names. Everything else it documents stays exactly as it is — the block order, the inline grammar, the `outline` output, the joining of blocks, and the failure wording — and the work spans the block grammar, the inline grammar, and the command.\n\nA bullet item may now be indented. Its level is the number of leading spaces divided by two and rounded down, capped at one deeper than the deepest item currently open in the list, and it is appended to the last item of the level above. A list still may not interrupt an open paragraph, and a blank line still ends the whole list. An item with children renders as `<li>` and the item's own text, then a newline, then the nested `<ul>` on its own lines, then `</li>` on its own line; an item without children renders as today, on one line.\n\nLink definitions join the block grammar. A line whose trimmed text is `[` a label `]` `:` then whitespace then a non-empty url is a definition wherever it stands outside a code fence: it ends any open paragraph or list, contributes nothing to the output, and records the label. A label is compared trimmed, with runs of whitespace collapsed to one space, and lowercased, and it is that normalized form that is written down and reported. Defining a label twice fails with `error: line N: label <label> is defined twice`, naming the normalized label.\n\nTwo reference forms join the inline grammar, both tried after `[text](url)` fails. `[text][label]` uses the written label and `[text][]` uses the link text as the label; the label is normalized the same way, and a resolved reference renders as `<a href=\"<url>\">` with the text rendered inline and the url escaped for the attribute. A reference whose label no definition resolves is not a link: the `[` is literal text and scanning continues after 